  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1101 PM MDT Wed Aug 12 2026\n\nModels are in good agreement with satellite imagery with troughing \nalong the West Coast and the high pushed off to the east over east \nTexas. This pattern puts eastern Utah and Western Colorado under a \nsouthwesterly flow pulling deep monsoonal moisture up out of Arizona \nwith pwats running over an inch across the region.Between the cloud \ncover and evaporative cooling, temperatures were ten or more degrees \ncooler today than what we've been seeing for the last week or so. \nThe limited heating did a lot to stifle convection. Tomorrow, \nThursday, looks to be very similar to today with the deep moisture \nand heavy cloud cover, but the flow aloft increases some to provide \nweak but better upper-level dynamics to support stronger showers and \nthunderstorms Thursday into Friday morning. Guidance is calling for \nmore widespread convective activity across the region. Concerns \ncontinue to be for areas of heavy rain and training showers and \nthunderstorms, especially over the recent fire scars where the risks \nfor flash flooding and debris flows are a high threat. Be sure to \ncheck your forecast before going out, have a way for getting alerts \nand stay aware of your surroundings.  \n\nThe continued southwesterly flow will tend to advect drier air into \nthe region into the weekend. coverage of showers and \nthunderstorms will become more scattered Friday favoring the higher \nterrain across the region and more isolated over the weekend. \nTemperatures rebound five to ten degrees by next Monday, but there \nare hints of another surge of moisture early next week from some \nmodels while others have us return to a drier pattern. Hence, \nforecast confidence drops off beyond a few days as slight variations \nin the patterns can significantly change forecast outcomes.",
